      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a8.23.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g8.23.7', 'g8.23.12'],
          lemma: "ἐκ τῶν κάτω … ἐκ τῶν ἄνω",
          title: "From below, from above",
          body: "The whole misunderstanding of the chapter is here in one line. They cannot grasp where he is going (v. 22) because they cannot grasp where he is *from*: he belongs to the realm **above**, they to the realm **below** — not two neighbourhoods but two orders of existence, the world “that knew him not” (1:10) and the God from whom he came. This is not a slur on their morals so much as a statement of their horizon: bounded by “this world,” they read a heavenly claim as either madness or blasphemy. To believe (v. 24) is precisely to be born “from above” (3:3) — lifted into the order he speaks from.",
        },
      ],
