      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a8.41.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g8.41.12', 'g8.41.16'],
          lemma: "ἐκ πορνείας … ἕνα Πατέρα",
          title: "“We have one Father — God”",
          body: "The argument has been escalating over *fatherhood*, and here it turns barbed. **“We were not born of fornication”** may be a sneer at the circumstances of Jesus’ own birth (cf. the whispers behind “we know his father and mother,” 6:42); and their proud claim, **“we have one Father — God,”** is exactly what Jesus is about to dismantle. His point is not about bloodlines: children resemble their father in *character* and *conduct*. To murder the one God sent, and to love the lie (vv. 40, 44), betrays a different paternity — and no ancestry, however holy, can override the family likeness written in what you do.",
        },
      ],
